Added several columns on Wazuh dashboard for improved visibility. The fields that I added for this exercise are:

***data.win.eventdata.ipAddress
data.win.eventdata.logonType
data.win.eventdata.commandLine
data.win.eventdata.parentImage
data.win.eventdata.targetUserName***


### data.win.eventdata.ipAddress
is the account that was targeted. On a 4625 it’s the username that failed to log in (baduser in your test). A single name repeated many times means someone is guessing that account’s password. Many different names from one source means username spraying.

### data.win.eventdata.logonType
is how the logon was attempted. It’s a number:


2: interactive (someone at the keyboard)
3: network (SMB, net use, file shares), which is what your loop produces
7: unlock
10: RemoteInteractive (RDP)


This matters for triage. Failed type 10 means someone is attacking RDP, and failed type 3 points to SMB or share attacks.

### data.win.eventdata.commandLine
is the full command that a process was launched with. It only exists on process-creation events (rules 67027 and 92037). For your test it shows net use \\127.0.0.1\IPC$ /user:baduser Wrong123, so you can see the exact credentials and target the attacker tried.

### data.win.eventdata.parentImage

is the program that launched that process. It shows the chain of execution. cmd.exe launching net.exe is expected for your test. winword.exe launching powershell.exe would be a classic malicious macro. Unusual parent-child pairs are one of the best signals in incident response.

###data.win.eventdata.targetUserName

 is the account that was targeted. On a 4625 it’s the username that failed to log in (baduser in your test). A single name repeated many times means someone is guessing that account’s password. Many different names from one source means username spraying.
